1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,650
Part five.

2
00:00:01,075 --> 00:00:02,312
The garbage-bag playbook.

3
00:00:02,738 --> 00:00:04,775
Cuban's father did car upholstery,

4
00:00:05,075 --> 00:00:07,488
and never made more than forty thousand dollars,

5
00:00:07,712 --> 00:00:08,688
as Bartlett puts it.

6
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,763
At twelve, Mark wanted new basketball shoes.

7
00:00:12,188 --> 00:00:12,875
His dad said:

8
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:13,662
get a job.

9
00:00:14,062 --> 00:00:15,800
A friend at the poker table offered him

10
00:00:15,812 --> 00:00:18,125
boxes of garbage bags for three dollars,

11
00:00:18,375 --> 00:00:19,425
to sell for six.

12
00:00:19,838 --> 00:00:20,850
Mark went door to door.

13
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:22,525
If you can sell door to door,

14
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:24,550
he says, you can do anything.

15
00:00:24,962 --> 00:00:26,212
Later, in Dallas,

16
00:00:26,388 --> 00:00:28,738
he shared a three-bedroom apartment with five other

17
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:30,388
guys and slept on the floor.

18
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:32,550
He got fired from a software store for

19
00:00:32,562 --> 00:00:34,212
leaving to pick up a commission check.

20
00:00:34,638 --> 00:00:35,975
So he started his own company,

21
00:00:36,275 --> 00:00:37,250
Micro Solutions.

22
00:00:37,675 --> 00:00:38,200
His edge?

23
00:00:38,625 --> 00:00:39,825
With any new software,

24
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,050
he figured only the person who wrote it

25
00:00:42,062 --> 00:00:43,525
would understand it better than him.

26
00:00:43,938 --> 00:00:45,762
Everyone else would be tied for second,

27
00:00:46,088 --> 00:00:47,000
and he would outwork them.

28
00:00:47,425 --> 00:00:48,950
He read the manuals through the night.

29
00:00:49,388 --> 00:00:51,262
He says it's exactly the same with AI

30
00:00:51,275 --> 00:00:51,675
today.

31
00:00:52,088 --> 00:00:54,500
And that company was profitable every single month

32
00:00:54,638 --> 00:00:55,488
for eight years.

33
00:00:55,912 --> 00:00:57,512
That's his warning to young founders.

34
00:00:57,925 --> 00:01:00,875
Raising money and chasing big sales numbers isn't

35
00:01:00,888 --> 00:01:01,312
the goal.

36
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:02,638
Staying alive is.

37
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:04,975
And he's blunt about who should even try.

38
00:01:05,388 --> 00:01:06,962
If you have to ask yourself whether you

39
00:01:06,975 --> 00:01:08,012
can be an entrepreneur,

40
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:10,188
he says, you probably aren't one.

41
00:01:10,625 --> 00:01:12,162
He sold Micro Solutions,

42
00:01:12,412 --> 00:01:14,262
then built Broadcast dot com,

43
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:16,838
which Yahoo bought for five point seven billion

44
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,788
dollars in stock.

45
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:19,587
For anyone stuck in a job,

46
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:21,087
his advice is simple.

47
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:22,800
Don't hunt for the perfect job.

48
00:01:23,212 --> 00:01:23,975
Just get a job,

49
00:01:24,262 --> 00:01:25,362
and get really good at it.

50
00:01:25,788 --> 00:01:27,762
Be brutally honest about what you're bad at.

51
00:01:28,175 --> 00:01:30,525
And ignore the get-rich-quick videos in your feed.

52
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:31,400
His line:

53
00:01:31,888 --> 00:01:33,462
nobody who has an edge in the stock

54
00:01:33,475 --> 00:01:34,525
market is selling it.

55
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:35,550
They're using it.

56
00:01:35,975 --> 00:01:37,562
Which brings us back to that crash.
